@page "/docs/troubleshooting/interactive-stops-responding"

@* A circuit lives on the server that started it; behind a load balancer with no session affinity,
   a later request reaches another server, which answers 404 and the circuit drops. IzzyBlazor's
   script keeps controls moving, so it looks like a component bug. Found on izzyblazor.com itself:
   a deploy left two Fly machines and the Slider's Live example stopped following the thumb. *@

<ArticlePage Section="troubleshooting" Slug="interactive-stops-responding">
    <Lead>
        An interactive page's circuit lives on the server that started it. With more than one server, send each reader back
        to the same one, or run only one.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Problem</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            An interactive page works on your machine, but once it's deployed, parts of it stop answering. Controls still
            move, since IzzyBlazor's script runs in the browser: a slider's thumb and value follow the drag, a menu opens,
            a dialog closes. But nothing that needs C# runs: a value bound with <Code>Live</Code> doesn't reach what
            follows it, and <Code>OnClick</Code> does nothing. It can work on one load and not the next, or stop after a
            while. Blazor's reconnection message may appear, unless the app hides it. The browser's console shows an
            error like this one:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@ConsoleError</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Because the controls still move, it looks like a bug in one component. It isn't: every interactive part of the
            page has stopped.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Cause</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            An interactive Server page runs over a circuit: the page's components and their state, kept in memory on the
            server that rendered it, and a SignalR connection to the browser. Every later request for that connection has
            to reach the same server. Behind a load balancer that spreads requests across servers, one of them reaches a
            server that has never heard of the connection. That server answers 404, and the circuit is lost.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Over WebSockets the browser holds one long connection, so this shows up less, but the request that sets the
            connection up and any reconnection can still land elsewhere. When WebSockets aren't available, SignalR falls
            back to long polling: a chain of separate requests. The page breaks the first time one reaches another server.
            Which server each request reaches is chance, so it fails sometimes and not others. The console says when the
            fallback happens: "Failed to connect via WebSockets, using the Long Polling fallback transport."
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Static pages don't have this problem: nothing is kept on the server between requests, so any server can answer
            any request. The servers still have to share their data protection keys, which protect forms, sign-in cookies
            and the state of prerendered interactive components.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fix</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Keep each reader on one server, or run only one:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Session affinity.</strong> Turn on the load balancer's sticky sessions, so it sends each browser back
                to the server it reached first: ARR affinity (Session affinity) on Azure App Service, stickiness on an AWS
                target group, cookie affinity on a Kubernetes ingress or Gateway controller. Fly.io has no affinity
                setting; it can route a request to one machine with the <Code>fly-replay</Code> header, but one machine is
                simpler.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Azure SignalR Service.</strong> It holds the browsers' connections and routes each one to the server
                with its circuit. Set its <Code>ServerStickyMode</Code> to <Code>Required</Code> for Blazor.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>One server.</strong> Fine for a small app, and the simplest fix. Some hosts start two by default:
                Fly.io's <Code>fly deploy</Code> creates two machines for a new app unless it's given
                <Code>--ha=false</Code>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="bash" Title="Fly.io">@FlyCommands</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Check that WebSockets get through too: a proxy in front of the app has to pass the <Code>Upgrade</Code> and <Code>Connection</Code> headers,
            or every page falls back to long polling, which is slower and makes this problem show up sooner.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A page that doesn't need C# on every click can be static, and then any server can answer it. IzzyBlazor's
            components post, link and run their script on static pages too.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Related</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what needs a circuit, and choosing a page's mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/onclick">OnClick does nothing on a static page</Anchor>: C# events need an interactive page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/host-and-deploy/server">Host and deploy Blazor Server</Anchor>: Microsoft's guide to scaling out, WebSockets and Azure SignalR Service.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string ConsoleError = """
        No Connection with that ID: Status code '404'
        """;

    private const string FlyCommands = """
        # See how many machines the app runs
        fly machine list

        # Go back to one
        fly scale count 1

        # Deploy without adding a second
        fly deploy --ha=false
        """;
}
